## Symptom

Running `c2c relay register --alias X` while the machine connector owns X's lease
**steals** the lease. The connector's subsequent heartbeats and polls for X then
fail `signature_invalid` forever (see the per-alias wedge bug), which in turn
feeds the machine-wide crash loop.

The relay's own error text tells operators to do exactly this.

## Reproduced

Before (connector owns nothing usable for this alias):

```
$ C2C_RELAY_NODE_ID=cli-codex-midst-vial-6nx9 C2C_RELAY_SESSION_ID=cli-codex-midst-vial-6nx9 \
    c2c relay dm peek --alias codex-midst-vial-6nx9 --relay-url https://relay.c2c.im
{"ok":false,"error_code":"signature_invalid",
 "error":"verified signer \"codex-midst-vial-6nx9\" does not own session
          (cli-codex-midst-vial-6nx9, cli-codex-midst-vial-6nx9)"}
```

Then one register:

```
$ c2c relay register --alias codex-midst-vial-6nx9 --relay-url https://relay.c2c.im
{"ok": true, "result": "ok",
 "lease": {"node_id": "cli-codex-midst-vial-6nx9",
           "session_id": "cli-codex-midst-vial-6nx9", ...}}
```

After — the `cli-*` key now owns it:

```
$ C2C_RELAY_NODE_ID=cli-... C2C_RELAY_SESSION_ID=cli-... c2c relay dm peek --alias codex-midst-vial-6nx9 ...
{ "ok": true, "messages": [] }
```

The lease moved from whatever held it to `cli-<alias>`, and the connector (which
signs under `(host_hash, local_session_id)`) is locked out.

## Contradictory operator guidance

Two hints in the codebase give opposite advice for the same error:

1. `ocaml/relay.ml`, `try_verify_ed25519_request` (B184 message) tells the
   operator, on a signature failure:
   `If you just renamed/re-registered, re-run: c2c relay register --alias %s`
2. The CLI `relay dm peek` hint says:
   `Do NOT run 'c2c relay register' - that steals the lease from the connector;
    the two then fight and delivery wedges.`

(1) is the relay speaking, is emitted more often, and causes the failure that (2)
warns about. Anyone following the server's advice makes the problem permanent.

## Secondary defect: no way to re-register under the connector's keys

`C2C_RELAY_NODE_ID` / `C2C_RELAY_SESSION_ID` are honoured by
`c2c relay dm peek|poll` (via `resolve_cli_dm_inbox_key`) but are **ignored** by
`c2c relay register`, which hard-codes `cli_inbox_key alias` =
`("cli-" ^ alias, "cli-" ^ alias)`. `c2c relay register --help` exposes no
`--node-id` / `--session-id` either. So once a lease is stolen there is no
supported command to hand it back; recovery depends on the connector happening to
restart with an empty `t.registered`.

## Fix direction

- Remove or rewrite the B184 hint so it never recommends `c2c relay register`
  when a connector may own the alias.
- Make `c2c relay register` refuse (or warn loudly and require `--force`) when
  `connector-state.json` shows a live connector owning that alias.
- Honour `C2C_RELAY_NODE_ID` / `C2C_RELAY_SESSION_ID` in `relay register`, or add
  explicit flags, so a lease can be deliberately returned to the connector's keys.

## Evidence

Full investigation: `.collab/findings/2026-09-17T01-50-00Z-kesa-quip-relay-connector-crashloop.md`

Lane: relay-core (see B293 note). Decision: add explicit --node-id/--session-id flags to relay register AND honour C2C_RELAY_NODE_ID/C2C_RELAY_SESSION_ID env (same override mechanism dm peek/poll already use).